<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="64512" href="schedule/2016/spring/GWS/295/64512.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2016" href="schedule/2016.xml">2016</calendarYear><term id="120161" href="schedule/2016/spring.xml">Spring 2016</term><subject id="GWS" href="schedule/2016/spring/GWS.xml">Gender and Women's Studies</subject><course id="295" href="schedule/2016/spring/GWS/295.xml">Beginning Topics GWS</course></parents><sectionNumber>LC </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Race and Criminalization</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>In order to examine how racial groups are criminalized, we need to delink race from crime. To do so, we need to question how laws are created and normalized. To do this effectively, I will ask you to suspend your moral judgments, so that you can approach crime, criminals, and criminal activity objectively and critically.</sectionText><partOfTerm>B</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2016-03-14Z</startDate><endDate>2016-05-04Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>09:00 AM</start><end>11:20 AM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>212</roomNumber><buildingName>David Kinley Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Cacho" firstName="L">Cacho, L</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>